WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Express routes should be grouped clearly
into public, authenticated,

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.001
privileged, service-only, webhook, and
administrative areas.

3
00:00:13.081 --> 00:00:17.725
Authentication answers who is making the
request.

4
00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:23.097
It might come from a session cookie,
bearer token, API key,

5
00:00:23.177 --> 00:00:29.766
identity provider, service credential, or
reverse proxy integration.

6
00:00:29.846 --> 00:00:34.382
Authorization answers what that identity
may do.

7
00:00:34.462 --> 00:00:39.646
These two checks work together, but they
are not the same.

8
00:00:39.726 --> 00:00:47.287
A logged-in user is not automatically
allowed to read every record or perform every

9
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.123
action.

10
00:00:48.203 --> 00:00:52.415
Consider a route that returns a document
by ID.

11
00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:59.299
The application must verify not only that
the requester is authenticated,

12
00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:05.536
but that the requester is allowed to
access that specific document.

13
00:01:05.616 --> 00:01:12.096
The same principle applies to organization
records, invoices, files,

14
00:01:12.176 --> 00:01:17.684
projects, administrative features, and
service accounts.

15
00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:24.677
Route-level authentication middleware is
helpful, but object ownership,

16
00:01:24.757 --> 00:01:32.749
tenant boundaries, roles, and business
permissions must be checked server-side close

17
00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:37.581
enough to business logic to understand the
context.

18
00:01:37.661 --> 00:01:44.682
Never trust client-supplied user IDs,
roles, tenant IDs, ownership claims,

19
00:01:44.762 --> 00:01:48.218
or admin flags as proof of permission.

20
00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:55.102
Treat them as request data that must be
checked against server-side state.

21
00:01:55.182 --> 00:02:00.583
Code review should look for routes that
bypass middleware,

22
00:02:00.663 --> 00:02:07.683
use inconsistent guard functions, trust
request fields for authorization,

23
00:02:07.763 --> 00:02:15.324
or handle service credentials differently
from human users without clear policy.

24
00:02:15.404 --> 00:02:23.180
Secure Express routing is about consistent
entry points and explicit authorization

25
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.340
decisions.
